er timeout → DONE ✅\n\nThose were all three items. Day 11 is a focused, targeted fix day. \n\nNow for Day 12, what should I do? Looking at remaining risks:\n1. The primary UX gap is no listening feedback — child has no visual indication the app is actively listening during the 8s window or how many attempts remain\n2. Synthetic audio still blocks real-device aesthetic QA\n3. App icon placeholder\n4. Looking at the spec — what's left?\n\nLet me examine the spec schedule more carefully. The project is about 5 days ahead, so Day 12 of the project aligns with spec Day 17+ content — app store submission prep and final polish. The most valuable work would be adding a listening indicator to show the child when the app is actively listening and which attempt they're on, since that's a critical UX gap for a 7-year-old user. The review confirms both Day 10 fixes are solid — `SpeechRecognizer` now properly times out after 8 seconds when no keyword matches, and the iPad landscape orientation is properly detected using `GeometryReader`. The integration test would catch any regression on device. The remaining 10% gap is three technical carry-overs (synthetic audio, programmatic icon, untestable alarm path) plus a real UX gap: kids get no visual feedback during the listening window that the app is actually listening or how many attempts they have left. Day 11's milestone on speech recognition is already complete from Day 5, so this review documents the spec §8 risk mitigation that was broken and is now fixed. The concurrency issue with `recognitionTask` callbacks accessing `@MainActor` properties from arbitrary threads is a pre-existing latent problem—not introduced by this change, but worth flagging since strict concurrency isn't enabled project-wide. The `HomeView` layout correctly uses `GeometryReader` to adapt the clock size between portrait and landscape on iPad while keeping iPhone unchanged, with a helpful comment explaining the dead code. The test properly guards against d